CH = dict(num=13, title="Earth as a System: Energy, Matter and Life", tag="Earth Science / Biology", theme="Spheres, solar radiation, winds, biogeochemical cycles, human impact",
 intro="Life on Earth runs on a constant flow of energy and matter. Treating the Earth as one system of interacting 'spheres', this chapter explains how uneven heating by the Sun drives winds and ocean currents, how water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen cycle through living and non-living parts, and how human activity disturbs these balances.",
 big_questions=["How does warming of the Arabian Sea affect the southwest monsoon?", "What happens to coastal cities if glaciers and polar ice melt faster?", "How would rising CO<sub>2</sub> affect ocean plankton?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "13.1 Earth as a System of Spheres"),
 ("table", ["Sphere", "Meaning", "Examples"], [
   ["**Geosphere**", "Solid rocks, soil, landforms, Earth's interior", "Deccan plateau, Thar desert"],
   ["**Hydrosphere**", "Liquid water: surface and groundwater", "Oceans, Ganga–Brahmaputra, lakes"],
   ["**Cryosphere**", "Water in solid form", "Himalayan glaciers, snow in Ladakh, polar caps"],
   ["**Atmosphere**", "Air surrounding the Earth", "Gases we breathe"],
   ["**Biosphere**", "All living organisms and their habitats", "Mangroves, forests, farms, plankton, coral reefs"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "A disturbance in one sphere changes others. Example chain: less winter snow (cryosphere) → lower lake level (hydrosphere) in summer → less grass (biosphere) for sheep. On a large scale: warmer Arabian Sea → more evaporation → variable southwest monsoon (floods in some places, drought in others) → warming melts glaciers and polar ice → sea-level rise threatens coasts and ecosystems."),
 ("h2", "13.2 Uneven Heating of the Earth"),
 ("h3", "Solar radiation and the electromagnetic spectrum"),
 ("ul", ["The Sun is the main energy source. Its energy arrives as **electromagnetic (EM) waves** that travel through vacuum at the speed of light, **3 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup>**. (Unlike sound, EM waves need no medium.)", "The spectrum runs from high frequency / short wavelength (gamma rays, X-rays — very energetic and harmful) through UV, visible and IR, to long wavelength microwaves and radio waves.", "About **99%** of solar energy is in **UV, visible and infrared**. Gamma and X-rays are mostly filtered by the upper atmosphere; microwaves and radio waves carry little energy."]),
 ("table", ["Radiation", "Role"], [
   ["Ultraviolet (100–400 nm)", "Mostly absorbed by the **ozone layer**; excess damages eyes and skin and raises cancer risk; also kills germs in water purifiers"],
   ["Visible", "Reaches the surface; energy for **photosynthesis**; partly warms land and water"],
   ["Infrared", "Warms the surface, which re-radiates heat; part of outgoing heat is trapped by **greenhouse gases** (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, water vapour)"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Insolation**: solar radiation reaching the Earth's surface. **Solar constant**: average solar energy received per unit time per unit area perpendicular to the Sun's rays at the top of the atmosphere, about **1.4 kW m<sup>−2</sup>** (1400 J s<sup>−1</sup> m<sup>−2</sup>). Because of absorption and scattering, maximum insolation at the surface is about **1 kW m<sup>−2</sup>** on a clear day."),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "Energy = intensity × area × time. &nbsp; Example: 1 kW m<sup>−2</sup> on 1 m<sup>2</sup> for 1 hour = 1000 × 1 × 3600 = **3.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> J** (≈ one unit of household electricity)."),
 ("ul", ["India lies in tropical and sub-tropical latitudes, so it gets plentiful sunshine — it drives the southwest monsoon and gives huge solar-power potential. **Anna Mani** mapped insolation across India (*Solar Radiation Over India*, 1981).", "Even a fraction of the Thar desert covered with solar panels could meet India's electricity demand (estimation exercise)."]),
 ("h3", "Surface effects: albedo and re-radiation"),
 ("p", "**Albedo** is the fraction of solar radiation a surface reflects (Latin for whiteness). High albedo means cool; low albedo means hot."),
 ("table", ["Surface", "Albedo", "Effect"], [["Snow", "0.80–0.90", "Reflects most; polar regions stay cold"], ["Ice", "0.50–0.70", "High reflection"], ["Crushed rock", "0.25–0.30", "Moderate"], ["Black soil, ocean water", "Low", "Absorb more; relatively warm"]]),
 ("ul", ["Dark roads and clothes heat quickly; light ones stay cooler. Concrete houses re-radiate heat at night, while thick mud-and-wood walls stay cooler.", "**Urban heat island effect**: cities are warmer than the rural surroundings because steel, concrete, brick and asphalt absorb and re-radiate heat; vegetation in rural areas gives shade and transpiration cooling. It raises air-conditioning demand."]),
 ("h3", "Latitude and Earth's shape"),
 ("p", "Earth is a sphere with a tilted axis, so the Sun's rays strike different latitudes at different angles. At the equator the same energy is concentrated over a **small area**; at the poles it is spread over a **large area**. Hence the equator is warm all year and the poles are cold. This temperature difference drives global winds and ocean currents and, with the tilt, causes seasons and changing day length."),
 ("h3", "Role of the atmosphere"),
 ("p", "Held by gravity, air is **78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen** with a little argon, CO<sub>2</sub>, water vapour and others. It has layers:"),
 ("table", ["Layer", "Height (approx.)", "Features"], [
   ["**Troposphere**", "0–12 km (highest over equator, lowest over poles)", "Nearly all weather; heated from the surface, **temperature falls with height (~6.5 °C per km)**; rising warm air drives winds and storms"],
   ["**Stratosphere**", "12–50 km", "**Ozone layer** absorbs UV so **temperature rises with height**; stable, little vertical mixing, so weather stays in troposphere"],
   ["Mesosphere, thermosphere, exosphere", "Above 50 km; outer space begins about 100 km", "Minor role in surface climate"]]),
 ("ul", ["Two protective roles: it **partly absorbs incoming radiation** (ozone, clouds, gases) and it **traps outgoing heat** through greenhouse gases. Without it Earth would be too cold; excess CO<sub>2</sub> from humans strengthens the greenhouse effect causing **global warming**.", "Venus is hotter than Mercury because its atmosphere caused a runaway greenhouse effect.", "**Ozone and CFCs**: CFCs from refrigerators and aerosols caused the Antarctic ozone hole; the **Montreal Protocol** curbed CFCs and the layer is slowly recovering.", "K. R. Ramanathan studied ozone at altitude and led early monsoon forecasting."]),
 ("h2", "13.3 Winds and Ocean Currents"),
 ("p", "Wind is air moving from high to low pressure; pressure differences arise from uneven heating."),
 ("table", ["Local wind", "When", "Mechanism"], [
   ["**Land and sea breeze**", "Day / night", "Land heats and cools faster than water"],
   ["**Valley breeze**", "Day", "Sun-facing slopes heat faster, air rises (low pressure) and cool air moves up from the valley"],
   ["**Mountain breeze**", "Night", "Slopes cool faster; cold dense air sinks down to the valley (felt in Shimla, Dehradun)"]]),
 ("h3", "Planetary winds"),
 ("ul", ["Intense heating at the **equator** makes warm air rise (**equatorial low**); it moves poleward aloft, cools and sinks near **30° N and S** (**sub-tropical highs**), and returns along the surface to the equator.", "Part of the surface air moves poleward, rises near **60° N and S** where it meets cold polar air (**sub-polar lows**). At the poles (90°), cold dense air sinks (**polar highs**) and flows to the sub-polar belts.", "Earth's rotation deflects winds: to the **right in the Northern** and to the **left in the Southern Hemisphere**, so they follow curved paths."]),
 ("h3", "Ocean currents"),
 ("ul", ["Continuous movement of large water masses. Planetary winds drag the surface water by friction; temperature and salinity differences, Earth's rotation and continents also shape them.", "Warm surface water flows from the equator toward poles; cold, dense water returns toward the equator at depth. Low-salinity water stays near the surface; **high-salinity dense water sinks**.", "Rotation produces circular **gyres**: **clockwise in the Northern**, **counter-clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere**.", "They carry heat from equator to poles (reducing temperature differences), carry nutrients for ecosystems and moderate climate. The warm **North Atlantic Drift** (extension of the **Gulf Stream**) keeps ports of north-western Europe ice-free in winter.", "IITM Pune runs coupled atmosphere–ocean–land–ice computer models to forecast the Indian monsoon."]),
 ("h2", "13.4 Biogeochemical Cycles"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Biogeochemical cycle**: the cyclic movement of matter (and energy) between the non-living (abiotic) and living (biotic) parts of the Earth, so that nutrients like carbon, nitrogen and oxygen are recycled and stay available for life."),
 ("h3", "Water cycle"),
 ("ul", ["Processes: evaporation, transpiration, condensation (clouds), precipitation (rain, hail, snow), run-off, infiltration and groundwater. Water dissolves minerals and carries them to the ocean.", "**Climate change effect**: warmer air holds more moisture, so heavier rain in some places (intense monsoon) and drought elsewhere; melting glaciers raise sea level (threat to Mumbai, Chennai); intense bursts increase run-off and soil erosion and reduce infiltration and groundwater recharge, hurting agriculture in dry months.", "The cycle links cryosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere, geosphere and biosphere."]),
 ("h3", "Carbon cycle"),
 ("ul", ["Carbon is the backbone of proteins, carbohydrates, fats and DNA (about 49% of dry weight of organisms). It moves between atmosphere (CO<sub>2</sub>), biosphere, geosphere (carbonate rocks, fossil fuels) and hydrosphere (dissolved CO<sub>2</sub>, shells).", "**Fast cycle (days–years)**: photosynthesis takes CO<sub>2</sub> into glucose; respiration, decomposition and animals' death return it.", "**Slow cycle (millions of years)**: buried dead organisms form coal, oil and gas; **burning** releases CO<sub>2</sub> quickly.", "Oceans absorb CO<sub>2</sub> as carbonate and bicarbonate ions used by phytoplankton and shell-forming organisms; about **71%** of global carbon is in oceans, only ~1% in the atmosphere.", "Burning fossil fuels and deforestation raised atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> by about **35% since 1960 (315 → 420 ppm)** — the Keeling curve with seasonal sawtooth from Northern Hemisphere plant growth. Excess CO<sub>2</sub> → stronger greenhouse effect, warming, melting ice, sea-level rise, extreme weather, more acidic oceans."]),
 ("h3", "Nitrogen cycle"),
 ("p", "Nitrogen is needed for proteins and nucleic acids. The air is rich in N<sub>2</sub>, but it is unreactive and cannot be used directly by plants and animals."),
 ("table", ["Step", "What happens", "Agents"], [
   ["**Nitrogen fixation**", "N<sub>2</sub> → ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>) (also nitrogen oxides by lightning)", "*Rhizobium* (legume root nodules), *Azotobacter* (soil)"],
   ["**Nitrification**", "Ammonia → nitrite (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>) → nitrate (NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)", "*Nitrosomonas*, *Nitrobacter*"],
   ["**Assimilation**", "Plants take up nitrogen compounds; animals eat plants/animals", "Plants, animals"],
   ["**Ammonification**", "Dead matter and waste → ammonia in soil", "Decomposers (bacteria, fungi)"],
   ["**Denitrification**", "Nitrates → N<sub>2</sub> gas returned to air", "*Pseudomonas*"]]),
 ("p", "The **Haber-Bosch process** (early 1900s) makes ammonia from atmospheric nitrogen for fertilisers ('bread from air'), enabling the Green Revolution, but it is energy intensive (1–2% of global energy) and fertiliser over-use degrades soil and water."),
 ("h3", "Oxygen cycle"),
 ("ul", ["Oxygen forms 21% of air and is part of carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and fats; also found in metal oxides, minerals and CO<sub>2</sub>.", "**Used** in respiration of plants and animals, in combustion of fuels, and in oxide formation; **restored** mainly by **photosynthesis** (sunlight + water + CO<sub>2</sub> → glucose + O<sub>2</sub>)."]),
 ("table", ["Cycle", "Main reservoir", "Key processes", "Human disturbance"], [
   ["Water", "Oceans", "Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, run-off", "Warming, altered rainfall, melting ice"],
   ["Carbon", "Oceans (71%)", "Photosynthesis, respiration, decay, burning", "Fossil fuels, deforestation"],
   ["Nitrogen", "Atmosphere", "Fixation, nitrification, assimilation, ammonification, denitrification", "Fertiliser over-use, eutrophication"],
   ["Oxygen", "Atmosphere", "Respiration and combustion vs photosynthesis", "Deforestation, burning"]]),
 ("h2", "13.5 Human Impact and Restoring Balance"),
 ("ul", ["**Ocean acidification**: excess CO<sub>2</sub> makes seawater acidic, threatening plankton and coral reefs; warmer water also absorbs less CO<sub>2</sub> (weaker carbon sink).", "**Eutrophication**: excess nitrates from fertilisers wash into rivers and lakes, causing algal blooms that deplete oxygen and kill fish.", "**Deforestation**: less photosynthesis and transpiration (lower local rainfall), changed albedo, soil erosion without roots, habitat and biodiversity loss.", "**Smog and ground-level ozone** from vehicle emissions harm health (stratospheric ozone is protective).", "Positive steps: Montreal Protocol (success), solar and wind energy, planting trees, saving water, sustainable farming, reduce–reuse–recycle; Kyoto Protocol and Paris Agreement have been less successful.", "**Mission LiFE** (Lifestyle for Environment), India-led initiative (2021), promotes mindful eco-friendly living."]),
 ("h2", "13.6 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("Name the five spheres of the Earth system with an example each.", "Geosphere (rocks), hydrosphere (rivers), cryosphere (glaciers), atmosphere (air), biosphere (forests)."),
   ("Why is the Earth unevenly heated?", "Because it is spherical and tilted, so rays strike latitudes at different angles; the equator gets concentrated radiation and the poles spread-out radiation; surfaces also differ in albedo."),
   ("Define solar constant and insolation.", "Solar constant: about 1.4 kW m<sup>−2</sup> at the top of the atmosphere; insolation: solar radiation reaching the surface (max about 1 kW m<sup>−2</sup>)."),
   ("Calculate energy on 1 m<sup>2</sup> in one hour at 1 kW m<sup>−2</sup>.", "E = 1000 × 1 × 3600 = 3.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> J."),
   ("What is albedo? How does it affect temperature?", "Fraction of radiation reflected; high-albedo snow stays cold, low-albedo black soil and oceans warm up."),
   ("Why do cities become heat islands?", "Concrete, asphalt and steel absorb and re-radiate heat, with less vegetation for shade and transpiration."),
   ("Why does weather occur in the troposphere?", "It is heated from the surface, warm air rises and drives winds, clouds and storms; the stable stratosphere above restricts mixing."),
   ("How do greenhouse gases keep the Earth warm?", "They trap infrared re-radiated from the heated surface; without them the Earth would be too cold."),
   ("Explain valley and mountain breezes.", "By day slopes warm, air rises and valley air moves up; at night slopes cool and dense air flows down."),
   ("What causes planetary winds and their deflection?", "Pressure belts from equator-to-pole heating; Earth's rotation deflects them right in the north and left in the south."),
   ("What are ocean currents and why are they important?", "Large steady movements of ocean water; they move heat and nutrients and moderate climate (Gulf Stream keeps European ports ice-free)."),
   ("Describe the steps of the nitrogen cycle.", "Fixation, nitrification, assimilation, ammonification, denitrification."),
   ("Why can plants not use atmospheric N<sub>2</sub> directly?", "It is unreactive; bacteria must convert it to ammonia, nitrite or nitrate."),
   ("Why is excess CO<sub>2</sub> harmful although plants need it?", "It enhances the greenhouse effect, causing warming, ice melt, sea-level rise, extreme weather and acidic oceans."),
   ("What is eutrophication?", "Algal blooms caused by excess fertiliser nitrates that use up oxygen and kill fish."),
   ("Mention two effects of deforestation.", "Reduced rainfall and photosynthesis, soil erosion and loss of biodiversity."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["Earth is an interacting system of **geosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, atmosphere, biosphere**.", "Solar radiation (UV, visible, IR) heats the Earth **unevenly** — by latitude, albedo and atmosphere; **solar constant 1.4 kW m<sup>−2</sup>**.", "**Troposphere** (0–12 km) has the weather; the **ozone layer** in the stratosphere absorbs UV; **greenhouse gases** trap heat.", "Uneven heating drives **local winds, planetary winds and ocean currents (gyres)**.", "**Water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen cycles** recycle matter between living and non-living parts.", "Fossil fuels, fertilisers and deforestation disturb cycles; **cooperation (Montreal Protocol) and sustainable living (Mission LiFE)** help restore balance."])
